  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nAs far as I can tell, the [rules for choosing a navigable](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-rules-for-choosing-a-navigable) indicate that code such as the following:\n\n```html\n<a href=\"foo\" target=\"named-window\" rel=\"nooopener\">Click me 1</a>\n<a href=\"bar\" target=\"named-window\" rel=\"nooopener\">Click me 2</a>\n```\n\nshould cause both links to open in the same window, named `named-window`:\n\n> Otherwise, if name is not an [ASCII case-insensitive](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-case-insensitive) match for \"_blank\", and there exists a [navigable](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#navigable) that is the result of [finding a navigable by target name](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#find-a-navigable-by-target-name) given name and currentNavigable, set chosen to that navigable.\n\nHowever, testing this shows that it always opens two separate windows. (Both get their `window.name` set to `\"named-window\"`.) I've tested the latest versions of Chrome, Safari, and Firefox.\n\nYou can use [this test page](https://boom-bath.glitch.me/spec-rules/target-hint-named-window.html), which also has some speculation rules stuff, but you can ignore that part of it.\n\nI guess we should fix the spec here so that the _noopener_ argument causes named windows to not be reused.\n\nNote: MDN apparently knows about this difference, and [says](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/open)\n\n> When noopener is used, non-empty target names, other than _top, _self, and _parent, are treated like _blank in terms of deciding whether to open a new browsing context.",
